## Purpose and scope

This review splits the date reports merged into #4471 by their underlying cause. It covers the 4 October export; it does not establish the current queue size or that a queued fix has shipped.

The [original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue and one fix. Its rendered cause table contradicts that claim. The table below computes the counts from unique report IDs, tenant locales, booking forms and channels, rather than copying intake guesses.

## Problems and tickets

- [Shared parser reads day-first dates month first](https://github.com/MarcinSzpunar/mega-s01e03-support-kb/issues/1). A tenant booking for 7 April becomes 4 July. Fix the parser's format contract.
- [booking-v3 date hint contradicts tenant-local parsing](https://github.com/MarcinSzpunar/mega-s01e03-support-kb/issues/2). Its MM/DD/YYYY hint tells day-first users to enter the wrong format. Fix the hint and field interpretation together.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The original ticket references above describe the exported desk state; the linked GitHub issues now provide separate follow-up tickets. Report IDs remove overlapping export rows. Browser locales and intake tags do not establish causes; the tenant directory and the form actually used do.

## Desk engineering update

The course desk's live update says #4471 closed on 5 October. It marks the parser **fixed**, with `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`, and the hint **won't fix** because the booking-v3 form was retired. The parser follow-up is closed as completed; the hint follow-up is closed as not planned. These are reported engineering outcomes, not independent production verification. The snapshot counts remain historical.

## Exceptions need investigation

<Callout>
The five reports noted below remain counted under parser by the supplied tenant-based classifier. Their bodies do not prove month/day reversal. Do not promise that either identified fix resolves them.
</Callout>

- R-41202: 12 May in the invite versus 11 May in the calendar.
- R-41209: every June booking is one day off.
- R-41236: an invite reportedly arrived before the booking existed.
- R-41344: the same meeting appears Tuesday in Lisbon and Monday in Toronto.
- R-41620: 6 October appears as Monday; the reporter says they have stopped reporting these incidents.

Check timezone/date-only handling and event ordering with the original bookings and delivery logs. These are unresolved symptoms, not evidence that a specific third cause has been confirmed. Declining reports cannot establish recovery when a reporter says they stopped reporting.

## Queue history

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Received time and merged time measure different events. A merge spike can reflect desk housekeeping rather than a new product regression. The chart ends at the export window; it is not a live health check. Compare incoming report rates, booking volume and user behavior before inferring improvement.

## Triage by cause

The [accepted subject-only decision](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) is historical and remains unchanged. Its rule can merge distinct causes with identical subjects and split one cause across different subjects. The current executable explorer compares causes instead:

<DedupeExplorer />

Use tenant locale, form version, channel and the report body to establish the same failure before merging. If evidence is insufficient, keep the uncertainty visible and investigate. A [replacement decision](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause) records this recommendation.

## Evidence and verification

Sources: reports-4471.json, the tenant directory, desk-notes.json, causes.ts, locales.ts, CauseTable, QueueTimeline and DedupeExplorer. Rebuild and read the rendered page whenever those inputs change. The static export is a snapshot while the desk continues working; refresh the evidence before claiming current totals or resolution.

